          ],
          "message": {
            "text": "NLST/MLSD/MLST/MDTM/SIZE bypass the -X dot-file read policy\n\nWhen the server is started with -X (dot_read_ok = 0), RETR and CWD call checknamesanity(), but NLST, MLSD, MLST, MDTM, and SIZE do not. An authenticated user can therefore list dot-directories, enumerate their entries, and stat dot-files even though the administrator prohibited reading them.\n\nSeverity: low\n\nCategory: Authorization policy bypass\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-863, CWE-200\n\nRemediation:\nApply the same checknamesanity(path, dot_read_ok) gate to NLST, MLSD, MLST, MDTM, and SIZE, or move the dot-file check into a shared path-resolution helper used by every filesystem command. Keep RETR and CWD behavior unchanged.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- With -X enabled, assert NLST, MLSD, MLST, MDTM, and SIZE reject .ssh and .ssh/known_hosts with the same policy error as CWD/RETR.\n- With -X disabled, assert the same commands continue to work.\n- Add a regression test that verifies a dot-directory cannot be traversed through a listing command.\n\nPreventive controls:\n- Centralize command-path validation so new filesystem commands cannot omit the dot-read policy.\n- Add protocol-level tests for every filesystem command against a dot-file fixture."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "codexSecurity/v1": "codex-security/v1:sha256:f26e9163e70b45df68865f22cd00045d3ddf538108446e6902472d3a9d666aa7"
          },
