          "message": {
            "text": "IPv6 EPRT error reply discloses uninitialized stack memory\n\nAn authenticated FTP client can send an IPv6 EPRT for an address different from its control connection. do_ipv6_port() passes an uninitialized sockaddr_storage to generic_aton(), which initializes only the family and address bytes. doport2() then formats the structure with getnameinfo() in the FXP-denial reply; glibc appends the uninitialized sin6_scope_id as a %N suffix, leaking four bytes of stale stack per request.\n\nSeverity: low\n\nCategory: Uninitialized memory disclosure\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-457, CWE-200\n\nRemediation:\nZero the sockaddr_storage before generic_aton() in do_ipv6_port(), as the IPv4 EPRT and PORT paths already do: memset(&a, 0, sizeof a). More generally, initialize every stack sockaddr structure at declaration and keep address construction in one helper that initializes the complete structure.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- Build with a nonzero sin6_scope_id left in a poisoned stack and verify EPRT mismatch replies contain no %N suffix.\n- Send EPRT |2|2001:db8::1|1025| after stack-shaping commands and confirm the reply is identical to the plain numeric address.\n\nPreventive controls:\n- Use a single address-construction helper that always zeroes sockaddr_storage before filling it.\n- Add a source-level regression test or compiler sanitizer run for EPRT IPv6 mismatch handling."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "codexSecurity/v1": "codex-security/v1:sha256:5eb9940d5a083ca57ef21a251031a644a7227055fc5c9aecfeec85dfb926d570"
          },
          "properties": {
            "category": "uninitialized-memory-disclosure",
            "confidence": "high",
            "findingId": "csf_2c5d2d0b63eeb94ba1ce7fe7",
            "occurrenceId": "occ_ddfe7b46af20d808ae12927d",
            "severity": "low"
          },
          "ruleId": "memory-safety.ipv6-eprt-uninitialized-scope-id",
          "ruleIndex": 2
        }
      ],
